  "id": "Compound::DB00788 - Gene::5742",
  "verbalization": "Naproxen is linked to Ptgs1 through a series of molecular interactions that involve the inhibition of cyclooxygenase (COX) activity. The evidence suggests that Naproxen, as a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID), exerts its effects by binding to and inhibiting the COX enzyme, which in turn reduces the production of prostaglandins. This mechanism is consistent across multiple paths, with Naproxen causing various adverse effects such as pseudoporphyria, infertility, and renal papillary necrosis, all of which are mediated through the inhibition of COX activity. The lowest common ancestors of these effects—Nonsteroidal Antiinflammatory Drug and Cyclooxygenase Inhibitor—highlight the shared pharmacological profile of Naproxen and its downstream targets. The integration of these pathways underscores the role of Ptgs1 as a key target in the pharmacological action of Naproxen, with the observed binding of Naproxen to Ptgs1 contributing to its therapeutic and adverse effects.",
